## 3. Pod Pending

**What it is:**
Your Pod is stuck in the "Pending" state and won't start.

**Possible Causes:**
- Insufficient resources on available nodes (CPU, memory)
- Resource requests for the Pod are too high
- Node affinity or scheduling issues

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Check node resource availability
kubectl describe node <node-name>

# Check pod scheduling issues
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

# Check available resources across all nodes
kubectl top nodes
```

---

## 4. Failed Mount

**What it is:**
A volume (like a persistent storage volume) can't be mounted to your Pod.

**Possible Causes:**
- No available Persistent Volume (PV) matching the claim
- Incorrect permissions or storage class settings
- Volume misconfiguration in YAML files

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Check PVC status
kubectl get pvc

# Describe PVC for errors
kubectl describe pvc <pvc-name>

# Check available PVs
kubectl get pv
```

---

## 5. Node Not Ready

**What it is:**
A node in your Kubernetes cluster is marked as "Not Ready" — it can't run any Pods.

**Possible Causes:**
- Network issues or loss of connectivity
- Resource exhaustion (out of CPU or memory)
- Kubelet not running properly

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Check node status
kubectl describe node <node-name>

# Check kubelet logs on the node
journalctl -u kubelet

# Check node resource usage
kubectl top nodes
```

