## 6. 503 Service Unavailable (Ingress/Load Balancer)

**What it is:**
Your service is unreachable, returning a "503 Service Unavailable" error. Typically happens with Ingress controllers or Load Balancers.

**Possible Causes:**
- Misconfigured Ingress or incorrect service routing
- Backend services or Pods not responding
- DNS or routing issues

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Check ingress configuration
kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name>

# Check if backend pods are healthy
kubectl get pods

# Check service endpoints
kubectl get endpoints <service-name>
```

---

## 7. Resource Quota Exceeded

**What it is:**
You've exceeded the resource limits (CPU, memory, storage) set for your namespace — no new Pods can be created.

**Possible Causes:**
- Namespace has reached its resource limits
- Unused resources consuming quota space

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Check current quota usage
kubectl describe quota -n <namespace>

# Check resource usage in namespace
kubectl top pods -n <namespace>

# Delete unused resources
kubectl delete pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
```

---

## 8. Kubelet Logs Filling Up Disk

**What it is:**
Kubernetes logs from Kubelet and other components are filling up disk space, potentially causing system instability.

**Possible Causes:**
- Logs aren't being rotated, accumulating over time
- High error rates or debug-level logs written frequently

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Check disk usage
df -h

# Check kubelet log size
journalctl -u kubelet --disk-usage

# Clear old journal logs
journalctl --vacuum-time=2d
```

---

## 9. API Server Throttling

**What it is:**
The Kubernetes API server is receiving too many requests and starts throttling — causing delays or dropped requests.

**Possible Causes:**
- Excessive or inefficient API calls from automation tools
- API server running out of resources (CPU, memory)

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Monitor API server resource usage
kubectl top nodes

# Check API server logs
kubectl logs -n kube-system <api-server-pod>

# Reduce unnecessary API calls in automation scripts
```

---

## 10. Pod Eviction

**What it is:**
Kubernetes evicts (removes) Pods from a node due to resource pressure — like running out of memory or disk space.

**Possible Causes:**
- Resource exhaustion (memory or disk space)
- Pods exceeding their defined resource limits

**How to Fix:**
```bash
# Check eviction reason
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

# Monitor node resource usage
kubectl top nodes

# Check pod resource limits
kubectl get pod <pod-name> -o yaml | grep -A5 resources
```

